Chapter: UnregisteredQuireCite
  cites Nonesuch chapter NoSuchChapter

 A cite naming a quire nobody registered is an error.

 This is the half that used to be invisible. missing-cite (beside this
 file) names a chapter that does not exist inside a quire that DOES --
 that always threw, because the resolver knew where to look and found
 nothing there. This one names a quire the registry has never heard of,
 and the cite pattern was built out of the registry's own keys: an
 unregistered quire did not match the pattern, so the cite was never
 walked, the chapter was silently left out of the unit, and the author
 got a cascade of CDX3002 at every use site and not one word about the
 cite. The check that would have caught the mistake was made out of the
 thing that was missing.

 A whole quire was lost that way and nobody noticed: codex/workflow's
 chapters were cited as Wflow, which was not in the registry, so every
 one of them was dropped from every unit that asked for them.

 The test is worth as much as its failure. Before the strict pattern
 this file compiled clean and printed nothing -- it would have passed a
 test that only checked for a diagnostic somewhere.

Section: Definitions

  opening : Integer = 0
